WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:04.710
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello, I'm
Dustin Kirkland, product

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.080
manager at Google, here
at KubeCon Copenhagen.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.510
And I'm joined by one of
my colleagues, Dan Ciruli,

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.610
product manager for Istio.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.670
Dan, every single
session about Istio

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.790
here is packed, people
falling out of the room.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:21.049
Why is there so much excitement
about the Istio project?

00:00:21.049 --> 00:00:23.340
DAN CIRULI: Well, I think
that the people who have been

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.910
adopting Kubernetes-- and
now, four years in, we've got

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.590
a bunch of people
who are using it--

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.256
have realized that
when you're deploying

00:00:30.256 --> 00:00:32.049
lots of things in
lots of containers,

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:34.298
there's lots of communication
between those containers

00:00:34.298 --> 00:00:35.760
and traffic into
those containers.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.286
And Istio helps them
with that traffic.

00:00:38.286 --> 00:00:39.660
When you've got
all that traffic,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.250
you need to monitor
it, see what's

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.760
going on in your application.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.829
You need to control it, where
all the requests are going,

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:47.370
and of course, you
need to secure it.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.830
And people realized, as
they adopted Kubernetes,

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.350
that there wasn't anything
in the ecosystem that did

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:53.790
all of those things for them.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:57.360
So Istio, they see us as what
will be the solution for all

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:57.986
those problems.

00:00:57.986 --> 00:00:59.235
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: That's great.

00:00:59.235 --> 00:01:00.870
So people talk about
the zero problems

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.780
of standing up a Kubernetes,
and we help with that with GKE.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.690
People talk about day
one, day two problems

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:07.669
getting those upgraded.

00:01:07.669 --> 00:01:09.960
What you're talking about is
really a step beyond that.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.126
Now you've got a Kubernetes.

00:01:11.126 --> 00:01:13.050
How do you keep the
interesting workloads

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.300
working on top of Kubernetes?

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.926
DAN CIRULI: Yeah,
really, it's giving

00:01:16.926 --> 00:01:18.800
a bunch of the tools
that are similar to what

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.964
RSREs have in operating
services at Google.

00:01:21.964 --> 00:01:23.880
One of our first-- in
fact, the first customer

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.020
to go into production on
Istio was Descartes Labs.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.860
They run a GKE.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.620
And they love the move
to micro services,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.650
but they found that after
they had adopted it,

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.560
when things slowed down and when
their app wasn't running well,

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.234
they had no idea why.

00:01:39.234 --> 00:01:40.650
And they had
multiple dependencies

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.900
between their services
and no real way

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.490
to figure out what
those were or which

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:45.990
ones were causing the problems.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.210
So they were an early
adopter of Istio,

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.550
and they instantly had a
service graph to tell them

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:51.900
where their dependencies were.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.740
But most importantly, they
had the golden metrics.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.115
They could see
what was going on.

00:01:57.115 --> 00:01:58.531
They could see
where the high load

00:01:58.531 --> 00:02:00.240
traffic was, where
the latencies were,

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.930
so they could
solve the problems.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.530
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So Istio must
touch a lot of different layers

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:07.200
in Kubernetes, certainly
networking, security, maybe

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.400
storage.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.070
Can you just talk a little
bit about Istio interacts

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.990
with the rest of Kubernetes?

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.390
DAN CIRULI: Well,
ultimately, it's

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.620
built on top of Kubernetes.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.240
We run our services
in Kubernetes.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.960
We're dependent
on the API server.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.830
And really, the
Kubernetes API server is--

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.190
everything that's building
on top of Kubernetes

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:29.070
is using the API server.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.440
We are replacing or
offering alternative

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.370
as an ingress, Istio ingress,
featuring the envoy proxy,

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.240
which is a really, really
fast, very powerful proxy

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.730
that a lot of people are using.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.610
And yeah, we're building on
top of that great Kubernetes

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:45.150
ecosystem.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.108
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: It's
all about the APIs, then.

00:02:47.108 --> 00:02:48.750
Having good, clean,
well-defined APIs

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.470
and adhering to those APIs.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.640
DAN CIRULI: Yeah, and what's
interesting about that

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.760
is that Istio itself helps
to govern those APIs.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.010
So I think these two projects
are going to go together

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.080
really, really well.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.455
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And where do you

00:03:01.455 --> 00:03:03.914
see Istio going next, as we
look out to the next six months

00:03:03.914 --> 00:03:04.413
or so?

00:03:04.413 --> 00:03:05.430
Can you give us a hint?

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:09.690
DAN CIRULI: Well, we're
at version 0.7 right now.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.270
We are fast approaching,
we hope, 1.0.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.400
We've got about 10
companies that we

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.940
know of in production today, and
so the code is getting there.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.570
We've got stability in our API.

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:21.540
So we're getting
ready to declare 1.0.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.206
And when we do that,
I think we're going

00:03:23.206 --> 00:03:24.425
to see a wave of adoption.

00:03:24.425 --> 00:03:26.050
And that's going to
be really exciting.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:27.895
We'll see the ecosystem
start to build.

00:03:27.895 --> 00:03:30.270
We've got a lot of partners
who are building integrations

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:31.020
right now.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.320
So I see us going from
very early adopters to more

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.980
mainstream adoption and a big
ecosystem building around it.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.290
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And what's
the last hard problems

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.080
to solve before getting
to that proverbial 1.0?

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.970
Can you give us a
sense of like, what's

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.720
the big thorny
issues on your mind?

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.220
DAN CIRULI: Performance
is an issue.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.970
We need to make sure that
people understand exactly how

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.700
much latency will this
add, how much more compute

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.290
is it going to add.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.960
We've got the APIs
pretty stable right now.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.350
We need to make sure
it is stable at scale.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:02.430
Although we do have--

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.310
weather.com is running it
at about 400,000 requests

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:06.450
a second.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.540
So we were already getting
into pretty good production

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:10.720
stability there.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:11.340
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
I was going to ask you

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.570
about scale, actually.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.270
You jumped one step
ahead of me here.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.300
But 400,000 containers-- all
right, sorry, 400,000 requests

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.580
per second is outstanding.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.404
Does this deal help stave
off some of the work

00:04:25.404 --> 00:04:27.820
that someone might have to do
otherwise, without a service

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:32.335
mesh in place to help with that
sort of scalability of traffic?

00:04:32.335 --> 00:04:34.710
DAN CIRULI: What it does to
your scalability of traffic--

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.770
it allows you to understand
your system better.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.200
Today, too many people
are scaling pods

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.000
on things like
memory consumption

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.040
or CPU, when in
reality, what you

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.182
might want to scale on
is the amount of traffic

00:04:46.182 --> 00:04:48.390
that you're getting or what
your latencies are doing.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.550
And so it's actually
giving better tools

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:54.140
to your operators to be able
to run those systems better.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.140
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Gotcha.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.770
So earlier today, we've just
heard about the Stackdriver

00:04:58.770 --> 00:04:59.430
announcements.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:02.907
Can you tell us about how Istio
and Stackdriver might interact?

00:05:02.907 --> 00:05:04.990
DAN CIRULI: Well, we already
have, in open source,

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:06.460
a mixer plug-in.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.920
Mixer's one of the components
of Istio that gathers telemetry.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.360
A plug-in today that will send
all of your Istio telemetry

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.740
to Stackdriver.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.420
We've got some really
interesting things

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.460
in the works with that
team right now, some

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.204
stuff that we can't talk
about, but they announced

00:05:21.204 --> 00:05:22.120
this Kubernetes thing.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.730
We're already working on the
next big thing with those guys.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:26.188
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
OK, last question.

00:05:26.188 --> 00:05:27.850
Security is on everyone's mind.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:31.690
How does Istio help improve the
security model, or the policy

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.910
model, around Kubernetes itself?

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:39.070
DAN CIRULI: It gives everyone a
standard way, and an easy way--

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:41.620
first of all, an easy way
to get MTLS on every call.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.480
We believe in a defense
in-depth strategy,

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.100
and even if your
perimeter is secure,

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:47.140
you should be securing
every single call.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:52.060
So MTLS lets you identify every
single call that gets made,

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.970
and that, in turn, lets you put
an authorization policy on it.

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:57.345
So it allows
everyone in the world

00:05:57.345 --> 00:05:58.720
to do what we do
at Google, which

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.120
is every single call is strongly
authenticated and authorized.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.830
And as I said, it's a
defense in-depth strategy

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:06.955
that can't be beat.

00:06:06.955 --> 00:06:08.320
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: That's
really well thought out.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:09.040
Thanks, Dan.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.040
Appreciate your time.

